{
  "result": "At dawn, the city seems to remember that it was once a landscape. Before traffic begins, the streets lie quiet as riverbeds, holding puddles, leaves, and the faint metallic shine of night rain. A baker unlocks a door. Somewhere above, a window opens, and the sound is small but decisive: another person has agreed to enter the day.\n\nI like these unclaimed minutes because they make ambition feel less like a command and more like a choice. In daylight, every sign advertises an urgency. Answer this message. Cross this threshold. Become measurable. Yet morning, before its machinery gathers speed, offers a gentler instruction: notice what is already here.\n\nThere is a sparrow balanced on a wire, shaking water from its feathers. There is steam lifting from a grate. There is an orange peel on the pavement, bright enough to look intentional. None of these things explains itself. Their value is not hidden in a lesson, nor in a future version of themselves. They simply occupy the world completely.\n\nPerhaps people need such examples. We spend so much time rehearsing arrival that we forget how to inhabit the road. We call a pause unproductive, although a pause can be the place where attention repairs itself. We call wandering wasteful, although wandering teaches the mind the shape of surprise.\n\nSoon the buses will sigh at corners, offices will bloom with screens, and the city will put on its public face. I will join it. I will make lists, miss chances, and try again.",
